# Auren — what never belongs in the repository.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: everything below is written by RUNNING the bot, not by writing it.
# A trace, a process id, a bot's memory of one world and an installed dependency tree are all facts
# about one machine on one afternoon. Committing any of them publishes local state, makes every run
# show up as a pending change, and — for anything under `player_memory/` — puts a real person's
# Minecraft name and the coordinates of what they built into public history.
#
# The rule these all share: if the bot regenerates it, git does not carry it.

# ── Dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `package.json` and `package-lock.json` are the declaration and they ARE tracked; `npm install`
# reconstructs the tree from them exactly. The tree itself is ~440 MB of other people's code.
node_modules/

# THE ONE VENDORED TREE THAT IS TRACKED, and it is not a dependency of the bot at all: `preflight`'s
# pass 4 needs a real JavaScript parser to decide whether an identifier is bound, and the instrument
# has to behave identically on both of the Architect's workstations. A parser reached through
# `npm install` exists wherever somebody last ran the install; this one travels with the repo, so the
# one test in the fleet cannot report OK on a machine where it checked nothing.
#
# WHY THE NEGATION IS HERE RATHER THAN IN THE ROOT `.gitignore`, where it lived until 2026-09-10: the
# workshop moved inside the bot, so this path is now under `Auren_Bot/` and THIS file gets the last
# word on it. A deeper `.gitignore` overrides a shallower one unconditionally, which means the
# `node_modules/` line four rows above would beat any negation written at the root. The rule that has
# to be beaten and the negation that beats it now sit together.
#
# WHY TWO LINES. Git will not look inside a directory it has excluded, so the negation has to re-admit
# the DIRECTORY before the contents can be re-admitted at all. The first line does that against the
# rule above; the second is what defeats the root's `**/node_modules/*`, which excludes every direct
# child of any `node_modules` in its own right — `espree/` among them — and would otherwise keep the
# contents unreachable even with the directory back in.
!Auren_Workshop/tools/vendor/node_modules/
!Auren_Workshop/tools/vendor/node_modules/**

# ── Run records ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every bot replaces its own trace file the moment it starts, so no file here ever describes more
# than one run — see the troubleshooting section of README.md. The folder is absent from a fresh
# clone on purpose: `js_kernel/utils/record_homes.js` creates it on the first line the first bot
# writes, so a placeholder committed to hold the directory open would be guarding nothing.
fleet_logs/

# Which processes are alive right now, by process id. True for minutes, on one machine.
fleet_control_runtime.json
# Which terminals the fleet has open (console_window's ledger) — the same kind of fact, for the same reason.
console_windows.json
console_windows.json.*.tmp

# ── A bot's memory of one world ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Buildspots, chest positions and inventories, keyed to the exact world that produced them. On any
# other world these are coordinates pointing at nothing. The design data beside them
# (`building_blueprints.json`, `crafting_blueprints.json`, `monster_tactics.json`) is authored
# rather than sensed, and stays tracked — the patterns below are narrow so they cannot reach it.
js_kernel/corporate_headquarters*.json
js_kernel/find_buildingspot*.json

# ── OTHER PEOPLE'S DATA — the one entry here that is about privacy rather than noise ────────────
# One file per person a contractor has worked for: their Minecraft name, and every building and
# workstation they own. Publishing it would publish them. Nothing sweeps these files, by design,
# so the only thing standing between them and a public repository is this line.
player_memory/

# ── Credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No secret is hardcoded anywhere in this project; the bot reads what it needs from the environment
# (`node start_bot.js --help` names every variable). This guards the habit of parking them in a file.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# ── Editor and OS droppings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/
npm-debug.log*
